The CPU backend offers its operations to stable-diffusion.assembly

Before the CPU backend computes a node, it calls sdasm_compute_forward(), which computes the node with stable-diffusion.assembly's kernels and returns true when they take it, and returns false, on every thread alike, when they do not; the backend then computes the node as it would have. When it plans a graph, it asks sdasm_work_size() first how much of the work buffer a node needs, which answers for the nodes the kernels take, and asks the extra buffer types after, as before.

The backend does not define the two functions, nor declare them itself: the code is stable-diffusion.assembly's (sources/hooks.cc), and the backend includes the header that declares it (sources/sdasm.h), which the project gives it as it builds the code into it. The compiler holds the calls here and the definitions there to the same declarations, which a declaration of the backend's own could part from unseen. A build without the project does not compile.

This is stable-diffusion.assembly's own patch, applied to the submodule by cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures; it is not for upstream. ggml's part is under the MIT License, and the patch's under the GNU General Public License, version 3 or any later version.

diff --git a/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c b/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
--- a/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
